<h2>Research projects</h2>
<ul class="bullets">
  <li><strong>The Belize–Guatemala territorial dispute.</strong>  Doctoral research investigating how claims about territory intersect with the ordinary experience of identity. Rather than treating the dispute solely as a matter of diplomatic or legal history, the project attends to the interpretations of people living within its wider social world, drawing on a qualitative, phenomenological-hermeneutic approach that reads the meanings of identity and belonging without assuming the political significance of a dispute is always explicitly voiced.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Working concepts</h2>
<ul class="bullets">
  <li><strong>Latentism.</strong> A working concept identifying the ways in which an unresolved dispute can govern conduct while withdrawing from explicit speech.</li>
  <li><strong>Demographic anxiety.</strong> A working concept referring to the conversion of population figures into claims about belonging.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Approach</h2>
<p>Phenomenology and hermeneutics guide an approach to social and political questions that begins with experience as it is understood and narrated. This methodological orientation is particularly important where public discourse and lived experience do not appear to move in unison.</p>
